  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Mild conditions most of the week.\n\n- Change to a cooler, moister pattern this weekend.\n\n- Snow potential coming late next weekend and early next week.\n\nWarm and dry conditions continue, for now, Friday while\ntemperatures remain 15 to 20 degrees above normal southerly winds\nincrease announcing a trough digging to our west. \n\nThere remains significant uncertainty in how fast change comes to\nthe Northern Rockies and how exactly that change manifests \nitself. One thing does seem certain, summer-like temperatures end.\nBy Saturday, 75% of all model solutions have high temperatures 20\ndegrees cooler than Friday and 25% of solutions forecast a 30 \ndegree change. Sunday's forecast suggests an additional 5 to 10 \ndegrees of cooling. One other component that seems dialed in is \nbreezy conditions pre cold front on Friday and then winds turning \nnortheasterly Saturday as a backdoor cold front moves through the \nregion.\n\nWhat is still very much in question, how much precipitation\nultimately falls, how low do snow levels lower, and how receptive\na warm ground will be to accumulation in the mountains. Along the\nContinental Divide is the best location for both precipitation \nand simultaneously snow levels at or below pass level.",
